Chapter 7. An HOC is a function that takes a component, does something, and returns a new component that renders the original.

```javascript
function withTheme(Component) {
  return function WithTheme(props) {
    const theme = localStorage.getItem('theme');
    return <Component {...props} theme={theme} />;
  };
}

const ThemedButton = withTheme(Button);
```

Before hooks this was *the* code-reuse pattern, so any codebase more than a few years old is full of them. You almost certainly shouldn't introduce new ones — a `useTheme` hook does the above better. But three jobs still have no clean hook equivalent.

## Use case 1: enhancing callbacks

Suppose you need click analytics on every button, dropdown and link in the app.

With hooks you'd visit every component, find what's attached to `onClick`, and edit each callback. Fine for two components, miserable for two hundred — and putting the analytics call inside `Button` itself mixes concerns and forces the data to arrive as props.

An HOC wraps the callback instead:

```javascript
function withLoggedClick(Component, eventName) {
  return function WithLoggedClick({ onClick, ...props }) {
    const handleClick = (event) => {
      sendAnalytics(eventName);
      onClick?.(event);          // still fire whatever the caller passed
    };

    return <Component {...props} onClick={handleClick} />;
  };
}

const TrackedButton = withLoggedClick(Button, 'button-click');
```

Feature code keeps using `onClick` exactly as before and never learns analytics exist. Add a prop to distinguish instances and it scales:

```javascript
function withLoggedClick(Component) {
  return function WithLoggedClick({ onClick, logLocation, ...props }) {
    const handleClick = (event) => {
      sendAnalytics({ location: logLocation });
      onClick?.(event);
    };
    return <Component {...props} onClick={handleClick} />;
  };
}

<TrackedButton logLocation="navbar" onClick={save}>Save</TrackedButton>
```

## Use case 2: lifecycle tracking

The same trick for mounts and renders — attach it once, apply it to anything:

```javascript
function withMountLogging(Component, name) {
  return function WithMountLogging(props) {
    useEffect(() => {
      sendAnalytics({ event: 'mounted', name });
    }, []);
    return <Component {...props} />;
  };
}
```

## Use case 3: intercepting DOM events

Global keyboard shortcuts on `window`, which dropdowns and modals need to suppress while they're open. Rather than sprinkling `stopPropagation` through every such component:

```javascript
function withSuppressedShortcuts(Component) {
  return function WithSuppressedShortcuts(props) {
    return (
      <div onKeyDown={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}>
        <Component {...props} />
      </div>
    );
  };
}
```

Any component that should block the global shortcuts gets wrapped, and nothing inside it changes. This is the general shape hooks can't reach: **a hook runs inside a component and cannot wrap that component's output.**

## Writing one correctly

Three things the naive version gets wrong:

```javascript
function withLogging(Component) {
  function WithLogging(props) {
    // 1. Forward ALL props. Swallowing unknown props breaks every caller
    //    who passes something you didn't anticipate.
    return <Component {...props} />;
  }

  // 2. Set displayName, or DevTools shows a tree of anonymous wrappers
  //    and profiling becomes unreadable.
  WithLogging.displayName =
    `withLogging(${Component.displayName || Component.name || 'Component'})`;

  return WithLogging;
}
```

**3. Forward refs.** A ref passed to the wrapper lands on the wrapper, so it silently does nothing. Pre-React-19 you need `forwardRef`; in React 19 `ref` is an ordinary prop and passes through with the spread.

Static methods aren't copied to the wrapper either — libraries use `hoist-non-react-statics` for this.

