Chapter 11. You know [what debounce and throttle are](/test-prep/15) and you can [implement both](/coding/13). Inside a React component they break in a way they never do in plain JavaScript — and the fix needs [entry 10](/advanced-react/10).

## The bug

```javascript
function Search({ onSearch }) {
  // BROKEN
  const debouncedSearch = debounce((value) => onSearch(value), 500);

  return <input onChange={(e) => debouncedSearch(e.target.value)} />;
}
```

Type "react" and you get **five** searches, not one.

`debounce` works by holding a timer id in a closure. Each call clears the previous timer and starts a new one. But every keystroke sets state somewhere, which re-renders `Search`, which runs the component body again — creating a **brand-new debounced function with a brand-new empty timer variable**.

Every keystroke debounces against a fresh instance that has no memory of the previous one. There is nothing to cancel. The debounce is decorative.

Throttle fails identically: a new instance is never in its cooldown window, so every call passes straight through.

## 📊 How It Works

