Chapter 13. [React Guide entry 24](/react-guide/24) explains what a portal *is*. This entry explains why they had to exist — the answer is entirely CSS.

## The problem

A dropdown rendered inside its parent:

```javascript
<div style={{ overflow: 'hidden' }}>
  <Button />
  {isOpen && <DropdownMenu />}     {/* clipped */}
</div>
```

`overflow: hidden` on any ancestor clips the menu. No amount of `position: absolute` or `z-index` escapes it — clipping is applied by the ancestor, and a descendant cannot opt out.

The second version of the same problem is stacking contexts. A modal with `z-index: 9999` still renders *behind* a sibling with `z-index: 2` when the modal's ancestor sits in a stacking context of its own. `z-index` only orders elements within their own stacking context, and new contexts are created by more things than most people expect: `position` plus `z-index`, but also `opacity` below 1, `transform`, `filter`, `will-change`, `isolation: isolate`, and `contain`.

That last part is what makes this so hard to debug — a `transform` added for an unrelated animation three levels up silently traps everything below it.

## Why the usual fixes fail

- **Raising `z-index`** — does nothing across stacking contexts. You can go to `999999` and still lose.
- **Removing `overflow: hidden`** — breaks whatever it was there for, usually rounded corners or scroll containment.
- **Restructuring the markup** — the menu has to live near its trigger in the component tree for the code to make sense, even though it must live elsewhere in the DOM.

That last tension is exactly what a portal resolves.

