Chapter 16. [Error Boundaries](/react-guide/25) covers what they catch. This entry is about the much larger set of errors they **don't**, and what to do about those.

## What boundaries actually catch

An error boundary catches errors thrown during **rendering, in lifecycle methods, and in constructors** of the tree below it.

```javascript
class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  state = { error: null };

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    return { error };            // render the fallback
  }

  componentDidCatch(error, info) {
    logToService(error, info.componentStack);   // report it
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.error) return this.props.fallback;
    return this.props.children;
  }
}
```

There is still no hook equivalent — a class is required, or `react-error-boundary`, which wraps one.

## What they miss

This is the important list:

| Not caught | Why |
|---|---|
| **Event handlers** | Run outside rendering — React isn't on the stack |
| **Async code** (`setTimeout`, promises, `fetch` callbacks) | The stack has unwound; the throw happens later |
| **Server-side rendering** | Boundaries don't operate during SSR |
| **Errors in the boundary itself** | It can't catch its own render error |

In practice most real errors are in that column. A failed fetch, a bad click handler, a rejected mutation — none of them reach a boundary.

