## Getting async errors into a boundary

The trick is to convert an async failure into a **render-time** error by routing it through state:

```javascript
function useErrorHandler() {
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  if (error) throw error;        // thrown during render — the boundary sees it
  return setError;
}

function Profile() {
  const throwError = useErrorHandler();

  useEffect(() => {
    fetchProfile().catch(throwError);   // async failure becomes a render error
  }, [throwError]);
}
```

`react-error-boundary` ships this as `useErrorBoundary`.

Use it deliberately. Not every failed request should blow away a subtree — a failed search deserves an inline message, while a failed "load the thing this page is about" deserves the boundary.

## Boundary placement

One boundary at the root turns any error into a blank page. Instead, place them where a failure has a natural containment:

```javascript
<ErrorBoundary fallback={<FullPageError />}>       {/* last resort */}
  <Layout>
    <ErrorBoundary fallback={<WidgetError />}>     {/* one widget dies alone */}
      <RevenueChart />
    </ErrorBoundary>
    <ErrorBoundary fallback={<FeedError />}>
      <ActivityFeed />
    </ErrorBoundary>
  </Layout>
</ErrorBoundary>
```

Rule of thumb: a boundary belongs wherever you can answer *"what should the user see if just this part fails?"*

## Reset and retry

A boundary that latches into its error state forever is a dead subtree. Give it a way back:

```javascript
<ErrorBoundary
  fallbackRender={({ error, resetErrorBoundary }) => (
    <div role="alert">
      <p>{error.message}</p>
      <button onClick={resetErrorBoundary}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )}
  onReset={() => refetch()}
  resetKeys={[userId]}      // auto-reset when this changes
>
  <Profile userId={userId} />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

`resetKeys` is the important one — without it, an error on one profile persists after navigating to a different profile, because the boundary has no idea the context changed.

## Composing with Suspense

Boundaries and `Suspense` are complementary: `Suspense` handles "not ready yet", the boundary handles "it failed". Order matters — the boundary goes outside:

```javascript
<ErrorBoundary fallback={<Error />}>
  <Suspense fallback={<Skeleton />}>
    <Profile />
  </Suspense>
</ErrorBoundary>
```

## The layers you actually need

Boundaries are one of five, and alone they're not enough:

1. **Error boundaries** — render-time failures
2. **`try/catch` in event handlers and async functions** — the majority of real errors
3. **Per-request error states** — a failed fetch usually deserves inline UI plus retry, not a boundary
4. **A global `window.onerror` / `unhandledrejection` listener** — the safety net for everything that escaped
5. **Reporting** — `componentDidCatch` sends the component stack somewhere you'll actually see it

```javascript
window.addEventListener('unhandledrejection', (event) => {
  logToService(event.reason);
});
```

